    "body": "### Problem\n\nWith `ui2` enabled and `cmdheight=0`, messages such as the one displayed after\npressing `u` are drawn over the statusline.\n\nNormally, pressing a motion such as `j`, `k`, `gj`, or `gk` causes the message\nto disappear and the statusline to become visible again.\n\nHowever, if `j` and `k` are mapped to `gj` and `gk`, respectively, the mapped\nmotions do not dismiss the message.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nMinimal config:\n```lua\nrequire('vim._core.ui2').enable()\n\nvim.o.cmdheight = 0\n\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'j', 'gj')\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'k', 'gk')\n```\n\nThen:\n\n1. Open a file\n2. Make any change\n3. Press `u` (the undo message is displayed over the statusline)\n4. Press `j` or `k` (the message remains visible)\n\nInterestingly, executing the motion through `normal!` does dismiss the message:\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'j', function()\n  vim.cmd('normal! gj')\nend)\n\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'k', function()\n  vim.cmd('normal! gk')\nend)\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe message should disappear, just like it happens when we manually press `gj` and `gk`.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-nightly+eb5c60b\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nnot applicable, I think\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS 26.11\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.48.2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\nnix flake (https://github.com/nix-community/neovim-nightly-overlay)",
